<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-dirac-delta" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Dirac Delta Distribution</title>
    <idx>dirac delta</idx>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Here we define the Dirac delta distribution and its relation to the unit step function.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <definition xml:id="def-dirac-delta-distribution">
      <title>Dirac delta distribution</title>

        <p>
          The <term>Dirac delta distribution</term> <m>\delta(x)</m>is a generalized distribution that equals zero everywhere
          except at the point <m>x=0</m> with a total area of one:
        </p>

        <md>
            \delta(x) =

                \begin{cases}
                    \infty &amp; x=0 \\
                    0 &amp; x\neq 0
                \end{cases}
        </md>
    </definition>


    <note xml:id="note-dirac-delta-interpretation">

        <p>
            Conventionally, the value of the integral of the Dirac delta distribution is unambiguous whenever the <em>support</em> of the delta function
            strictly lies inside or outside the bounds of the interval over which the integral is being taken.
            Ambiguity arises when the delta spike occurs precisely at the bounds of the interval.
        </p>
        <p>
            For the purposes of :logo: <em>There and Back Again</em>, we will mathematically represent this nuance as follows:
        </p>

        <md>
            \int^b_a {\delta(x-c) \, dx}

                = \begin{cases}
                    0 &amp; \text{if } c\in(-\infty,a) \cup (b,\infty) \\
                    1 &amp; \text{if } c\in(a,b) \\
                    * &amp; \text{if } c\in {a,b}
                   \end{cases}
        </md>

        <dl>
            <title>Endpoint conventions</title>

            <li>
                <title><em>full-contribution convention</em></title>

                <md>
                    * = 1
                </md>
            </li>

            <li>
                <title><em>symmetric convention</em></title>

                <md>
                    * = \frac{1}{2}
                </md>

                <p>
                   Induced by the symmetric Heaviside step function <m>H(x)</m> where <m>H(0)=\tfrac{1}{2}</m> .
                </p>
            </li>

        </dl>

        <note>
            <p>
                This is an example of a statement whose value depends on an
                interpretive convention rather than solely on formal manipulation.
                The distinction appears because the Dirac delta is a distribution,
                not an ordinary function.
            </p>
        </note>

    </note>



</subsection>